---
import '../styles/global.css';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import ThemeInit from '../components/ThemeInit.astro';
import '../styles/graphs.css';
import Anatomy from '../components/graphs/Anatomy.astro';
import Story from '../components/graphs/Story.astro';
import { storyAssumption, storyLostOption, storyRevise, storyWho } from '../data/graph-stories';
import { DEMO_URL } from '../data/demo';
import { founders } from '../data/founders';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
// The version check is part of step 1: on the landing path, `claude mcp add` succeeds without the
// binary on PATH and Claude Code only fails later (ENOENT); the fix is in the Quickstart, linked here.
const installCmd = 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexknips/loomtracer/master/scripts/install.sh | sh\nloomtracer --version';
// --agent-tool claude files captures under Claude Code; without it the server files them under codex.
const connectCmd = 'claude mcp add loomtracer -- loomtracer mcp --agent-tool claude';
// Everything on this page works in the current release (v0.8.0, 23 Oct 2026), so it carries no
// status marks; what comes next and what is planned is told once, on the use cases page.
// Every claim on this page is listed with its evidence in CLAIMS.md at the repo root.
// The page tells the problem (the next session undoes it, nobody knows why, nobody can say if it
// held up) once, in #problem, and goes straight on to what it is useful for (the founder's review of
// 2026-09-30: too much duplication, too much text, get to the use cases sooner). Since 2026-10-02 it
// keeps only what works today, about 1,000 words. The real Claude Code session lives in the deck.
// The product is Loomtracer since 2026-10-07, and its command-line tool is `loomtracer` since v0.8.0
// (2026-10-23; it was `hivemind`), so every command uses that name and step 1 says so once. The founders' block (#contact) closes the page.
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>Loomtracer — memory for decisions</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Loomtracer is memory for decisions. Your agents record what was decided, which options lost and why, and who decided, you or the agent. Local-first, AGPL-3.0, free to self-host." />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} />
  <ThemeInit />
</head>
<body class="bg-background text-foreground antialiased">

  <SiteHeader />

  <main>

    <!-- Hero: the headline, one line and the two buttons. The badge says the release and local-first,
         the install section says self-hosted, so the hero does not. -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-14 sm:pt-20 pb-12 sm:pb-16 text-center">
      <p class="badge badge-outline mb-8">
        <span class="size-1.5 rounded-full bg-success" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        v0.8.0 &middot; open source &middot; local-first
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-6">
        Loomtracer is memory<br class="hidden sm:inline" /> <span class="text-muted-foreground">for decisions.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="text-md sm:text-lg text-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10 leading-relaxed">
        Your agent forgets what you decided together. Loomtracer keeps it.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center">
        <a href="#self-host" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
          Self-host it &rarr;
        </a>
        <a href={DEMO_URL} class="btn btn-outline btn-lg">
          Open the demo &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The problem, told once, in plain words (the founder's direction of 2026-09-29). The one place on
         the page where "held up" is a heading, with the note that it means contested or replaced. -->
    <section id="problem" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-10 sm:mb-12">Decide something with an agent, and three things go wrong</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 sm:gap-10">
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">The next session undoes it</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              You ruled an option out, for a reason. A fresh session never heard of it and suggests it again.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              Loomtracer keeps the options that lost, and why they lost.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Nobody knows why</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The chat was the meeting. Once it closes, nobody can say why, or who made the call.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              Loomtracer keeps the reasons, the evidence and who decided: you or the agent.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Nobody can say if it held up</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Plans change. The notes still say the old thing, and the agent follows them.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              In Loomtracer a replaced decision reads <span class="badge badge-neutral">superseded</span> and a disputed one
              <span class="badge badge-warning">contested</span>. Nobody has to remember to update it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-3">
              "Held up" means just that, contested or replaced. It is not a score.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The feature showcase, right after the opening (the founder, 2026-09-30: "What it is useful for is
         amazing and we need to get to it sooner"). One feature per card; the card's graph is its example,
         so the cards carry no second telling in prose. Only what works today (2026-10-02): revised or
         replaced, who decided, what it rests on, why with the options that lost. The bet, the questions
         still waiting, the seven dimensions, the drifting projects and speed with consistency and
         flexibility are on the use cases page, each told once. Reordering is moving a card; CLAIMS.md
         lists the evidence. -->
    <section id="showcase" class="border-t border-border bg-sidebar scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">Use cases</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-3">What it is useful for</h2>
        <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-12 sm:mb-16">
          Everything on this page works in v0.8.0.
        </p>

        <Anatomy />

        <div class="space-y-6">

          <!-- A decision is revised or replaced, and its status changes -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Change your mind, and the old decision says so</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                The old decision stays, pointing to what replaced it. Nothing is edited or deleted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyRevise} />
          </article>

          <!-- Who decided: you or the agent -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Who decided: you or the agent</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Every decision says who made it, a person or an agent, apart from who wrote it down. It is for seeing
                your own decisions, never a score of anyone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyWho}>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground flex flex-wrap lg:flex-col gap-x-5 gap-y-2 mt-5 lg:mt-0">
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--you" aria-hidden="true"></span> you decided</span>
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--delegated" aria-hidden="true"></span> an agent, within what you handed it</span>
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--agent" aria-hidden="true"></span> an agent, on its own</span>
                <span>The record holds who decided; the colours are this drawing's.</span>
              </p>
            </Story>
          </article>

          <!-- What a decision rests on -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">What a decision rests on</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Every decision names what it rests on: an earlier decision, something observed, an assumption, or a bet
                with a date to check. When that is refuted or replaced, the decision says so.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyAssumption} />
          </article>

          <!-- Decisions are remembered with the options that lost, and why -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Ask why, and get the options that lost</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                In a fresh session, ask why, and get the reason, the options that lost, who decided, and whether it
                still holds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyLostOption} />
          </article>

        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The <a href={`${base}use-cases/#next`} class="link">use cases page</a> has what comes next and what is
          planned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who it is for and how it compares, one short section (vision/README.md: "Who it is for", the
         shelf, what we do not claim yet). "Not only for engineering" (the founder's direction of
         2026-09-29) and "one person and their agents" (2026-09-30) live here since the hero is one line.
         The old anchors #how-it-compares and #where-it-is stay for old links. -->
    <section id="who-it-is-for" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">Who it is for</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-8">One person and their agents</h2>
        <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Anyone who decides things with agents every day. Not only for engineering: a plan, a hire, a supplier, a
          verdict. Today it takes a developer's setup: you run it yourself and connect your agent over MCP.
          <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Soon to come:</span> teams on one shared record, not built yet.
        </p>
        <h3 id="how-it-compares" class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mt-10 mb-3 scroll-mt-20">Against what you do today</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
          <li><span class="font-medium text-foreground">A hand-kept <code class="code">DECISIONS.md</code> or ADRs</span> are only as true as their last edit. In Loomtracer the status follows from later decisions.</li>
          <li><span class="font-medium text-foreground">Agent memory tools</span> remember facts and preferences. Loomtracer remembers decisions: the options that lost, the evidence, who decided.</li>
        </ul>
        <p id="where-it-is" class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-6 scroll-mt-20">
          Not claimed yet: that it makes your decisions better (a side-by-side test is planned), that anyone outside
          uses it, or that it recalls better than memory tools.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How to start: self-host (the deck's last slide) -->
    <section id="self-host" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">How to start</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-3">Start on your own machine</h2>
        <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-12">
          No account, no API key, and nothing phones home.
        </p>

        <ol class="border-y border-border divide-y divide-border">
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">1</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Install the command-line tool, <code class="code">loomtracer</code></h3>
              <pre class="code-block mb-3"><code>{installCmd}</code></pre>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Linux (x86_64, ARM64) and Apple Silicon Macs; the script checks the release&rsquo;s SHA-256. Other
                systems, or <code class="code">loomtracer: not found</code>: see the
                <a href={`${base}getting-started/quickstart/#if-loomtracer-is-not-found`} class="link">quickstart</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">2</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Connect your agent</h3>
              <pre class="code-block mb-3"><code>{connectCmd}</code></pre>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Run it in your project for Claude Code. Decisions go to <code class="code">./loomtracer/</code>,
                a SQLite ledger you own. Codex, Cursor and Claude Desktop are in
                <a href={`${base}guides/mcp-setup/`} class="link">MCP setup</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">3</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Record a decision, then ask why</h3>
              <div class="code-block font-sans text-base whitespace-normal break-normal space-y-2 mb-3">
                <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">You:</span> Record a decision: we use SQLite for the ledger, not Postgres, which is more than we need. It rests on one machine being enough.</p>
                <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">Later:</span> Why did we pick SQLite, and what did we rule out?</p>
              </div>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                The <a href={`${base}pitch/`} class="link">deck</a> shows a real Claude Code session recording one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-8">
          Prefer a server to a local file?
          <a href={`${base}getting-started/quickstart/#run-a-cell-with-docker`} class="link">Run a Loomtracer cell with Docker Compose</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Contact: the founders, as the deck's last slide names them, with their GitHub profiles
         (src/data/founders.ts; the footer lists them too). No email addresses. -->
    <section id="contact" class="border-t border-border bg-sidebar scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">Contact</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-3">Talk to the founders</h2>
        <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-10">
          Questions, ideas, or a place where Loomtracer could fit your work: find us on GitHub.
        </p>
        <ul class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {founders.map((f) => (
            <li class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 min-w-0">
              <p class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground">{f.name}</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">{f.role}</p>
              <a href={`https://github.com/${f.github}`} class="link text-sm">github.com/{f.github}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

</body>
</html>
